A||||9||Fret Naught||Interior Heat||||5||
Z||000000||Fret Naught||07-18-2000||07:40 PM||||So, I am crusing down the highway 70-75 it is about 80-85 degrees outside, and the inside of my car is just cooking, got the vents open, drivers side window open and sunroof open, seems to get real warm from my waist on down. Whats up with that? the engine is in the back, seems like the heat is originating from the center hump? Any ideas, or am I just imagining it? Almost forgot, I don't have A/C.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||07-18-2000||08:50 PM||||Temp's in my area have been right around 100 for the past few days and in an effort to try and beat the heat, I made a few additions to the Demon (88gt). First, I got a piece of that corregated (sp?) plastic sign material- it looks like cardboard but is made of plastic. With this I made my own sunroof shade. I just trimmed the stuff down until it sat in the little ledge where the headliner is attached. It is white plastic and seems to reflect a LOT of the heat. Eventually I will get the plastic covered in grey cloth to match the interior. Next, I put a strip of the darkest limo tint I could find along the top of my windshield. It aint pretty, but every little bit helps!||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||07-18-2000||09:00 PM||||Great tip Cooter!!!<P>Don't cover the glass side of your board, as flat white reflects the most light/heat. More even than a chromed surface.<P>My roof has a reflective tint which cuts down the heat. Coupled with your white board would be the way to go, me thinks.<P>F/N Could that heat be comming from the radiator via the tunnel? Don't know of any other source except the heater core. Is that open?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Uaana||07-19-2000||01:28 AM||||Fred just my 2c.  But when I'm being cheap or enjoying the fresh air, I find the interior stays cooler with the windows down sunroof open and the vents "closed" for some reason on the really hot days the vents just feel like sitting in front of a blow dryer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fret Naught||07-19-2000||01:36 AM||||Is that where the radiator lines run? I thought they ran on the out side edges of the car, The thing that sort of caused me to ask the question is that it wasn't that hot out maybe 85 at the very top, I could stick my hand out the sun roof, and feel the air was cool, that is why I turned on the vents, figured I could route some of that cool air inside.  but if the radiator lines do run down the tunnel, that would explain it. I need to pull the console off and see if there is a need for some insulation. I have no idea where the heater core is located, where ever it is, I bet it is a real B!@$H to get too.||||reg||6
Z||000005||StuGood||07-19-2000||01:55 AM||||You'd lose that bet, Fret!<P>Heater core is on a Fiero is so easy to get at, it's almost as if someone designed it that way!  Four bolts (7mm socket) allow removal of a cover on RHS of HVAC box ("Blue Dingy Thingy" area).  Look under the cover, and heater core is staring you in the face  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> .     ||||reg||12
Z||000006||Standard||07-19-2000||04:35 AM||||Heat from the engine compartment has a tendancy to come in through the center console, from the holes for the ECM wires and shift cables.  Take out an ashtray and stick your hand down there during a drive, you'd be surprised at how warm it gets in there.<P>In fact, I read somewhere that you should always have the windows open if you have the sunroof off, because with the windows closed and the sunroof off the cabin becomes a "low pressure area" and has more of a tendancy to suck hot air from the engine compartment, right past the ECM which could overheat the ecm and lead to weird problems.  I've never actually heard of this happening, but i guess it could..<P>Oh, and the radiator lines are those silver tubes right under the doors.  The gas tank is underneath the center console.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||07-19-2000||08:34 AM||||I found that if the sunroof is up and just the left window is open, sometimes I can feel heat hitting my forehead. I think the air flow pattern with that combination might be bringing the air from over the engine in to the cabin. If I have both windows open and the sunroof up, it seems to alleviate this problem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||07-19-2000||10:15 AM||||a removeable rear window like the first couple of years of c3 vettes would be nice if it could be worked up.<P>i don't use the vents when it's warm, they're no help.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||07-19-2000||01:03 PM||||Fred,<P>Have you checked to see that the heater/AC controls aren't keeping the heater door open a little?  In Birmingham, AL it has been in the low 100s for about two weeks.  I drive home on a mountain road in the early evening when it is still in the upper 90s and I don't get the same effect you describe.<P>I only open the driver's window and the sunroof since I haven't installed the PW/PDL yet.||||reg||1
